## 12. Confirm Dialog Pattern

Use confirm dialogs for destructive or irreversible actions.

```tsx
<AlertDialog>
  <AlertDialogTrigger asChild>{trigger}</AlertDialogTrigger>
  <AlertDialogContent>
    <AlertDialogHeader>
      <AlertDialogTitle>{title}</AlertDialogTitle>
      <AlertDialogDescription>{description}</AlertDialogDescription>
    </AlertDialogHeader>
    <AlertDialogFooter>
      <AlertDialogCancel>Cancel</AlertDialogCancel>
      <AlertDialogAction>{confirmText}</AlertDialogAction>
    </AlertDialogFooter>
  </AlertDialogContent>
</AlertDialog>
```

Required for:

```txt
reject application
cancel application
mark payment as paid
disable custom frontend
delete/deactivate records later
```

---

## 13. Empty, Loading, Error

Use simple reusable states.

Empty state:

```tsx
export function EmptyState({
  title,
  description,
}: {
  title: string;
  description?: string;
}) {
  return (
    <div className="flex flex-col items-center justify-center rounded-lg border border-dashed p-8 text-center">
      <h3 className="font-medium">{title}</h3>
      {description ? (
        <p className="mt-1 text-sm text-muted-foreground">{description}</p>
      ) : null}
    </div>
  );
}
```

Route loading:

```tsx
export default function Loading() {
  return <div className="space-y-3">{/* skeletons */}</div>;
}
```

Route error:

```tsx
"use client";

export default function Error({ reset }: { reset: () => void }) {
  return (
    <main>
      <h2>Something went wrong.</h2>
      <button onClick={reset}>Try again</button>
    </main>
  );
}
```

Rules:

- Do not show raw internal error messages to public users.
- Admin errors can be more specific but should still avoid leaking secrets.
- Keep loading states simple until real UI is built.

---

## 14. Activity / Audit Timeline

For admin workflows, prefer audit logs over purely decorative timelines.

Good uses:

```txt
application submitted
application reviewed
payment confirmed
client provisioned
onboarding email sent
Meta CAPI purchase attempted
custom frontend connected later
```

Start with a simple list/table. Add animation later only if it improves clarity.

---

## 15. Delete vs Archive

For RSVP MVP, avoid hard delete by default.

Preferred:

```txt
cancel application
reject application
archive event
disable custom frontend
expire hosting/access
```

Hard delete only when:

```txt
record is a test/dev artifact
privacy/legal request requires deletion
admin explicitly confirms permanent removal
```

Always audit destructive actions.

---

## 16. TanStack Query / Table Escalation Rule

Start simple.

Add TanStack Query only when:

```txt
client-side refetch/invalidation is needed
interactive tables become hard with Server Components alone
dashboard sections need live-ish refresh
```

Add TanStack Table only when:

```txt
sorting/filtering/selection/column visibility becomes complex
same table framework will be reused across several admin pages
```

Do not add either library during scaffold or first schema planning.

---

## 17. Anti-Patterns

Avoid:

```txt
route-local actions.ts scattered across every page
business logic inside components
service-role Supabase in UI components
raw Supabase errors shown to public users
client-provided payment status
hard delete by default
TanStack Table for simple tables
TanStack Query for simple Server Component reads
Zustand for table filters
Motion animations in admin CRUD by default
copying POS CRUD structure into RSVP
old @webserbisyo/* imports
```

---

## 18. MVP CRUD Implementation Order

Use this order:

```txt
1. Applications list
2. Application detail
3. Application approve/reject actions
4. Client list
5. Client detail
6. Payment records
7. Email logs
8. Audit logs
9. Meta Pixel settings
10. Sales summary
```

After CRUD changes, run:

```bash
npm run format:check
npm run lint
npm run typecheck
npm run build
```